.TH "git-gud" 1

.SH NAME
git-gud - update the main branch and remove branches deleted from the remote

.SH SYNOPSIS
git gud

.SH DESCRIPTION
Fetches the remote of the default branch (main or master, see
\fBgit mainbranch\fR) with --prune, then fast-forwards the local default branch
to its upstream. The default branch is never checked out for this. If it is
checked out in any worktree it is merged with --ff-only in that worktree,
otherwise the branch ref is moved directly.

If the current branch has an upstream which still exists, it is updated with
\fBgit pull --rebase\fR. If the upstream of the current branch is gone, the
default branch is checked out. If the default branch is checked out in another
worktree, a new branch forked from it is created and checked out instead, named
like main-1 or master-1 with the first unused number.

Any local branches that match branches which were deleted from the remote during
the fetch are then deleted, even if they have unmerged changes.

If a deleted branch is checked out in another worktree, the worktree is removed
first. Worktrees with modified or untracked files are not removed, and the
branch is left in place. These are reported at the end and the command exits
non-zero. The remote tracking branch is already pruned, so a later run will not
retry them; clean them up manually.

If there is a remote named "upstream" it is fetched as well and the local
default branch is fast-forwarded to the default branch on the upstream, then
pushed to its own remote. A failure to update any branch or sync with upstream
does not prevent cleaning up pruned branches, but causes a non-zero exit.
